WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:06.490
Jack Black returns as Po, the world's most
awesome martial arts practising panda.

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:12.830
I'm Jan and this is 27 Awesome Things You
Probably Didn't Know About Kung Fu Panda 3!

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:18.130
To play the awesome Kung Fu Panda Po, Jack
Black says he channels his younger self when

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:20.180
he was just starting out as an actor.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.910
Black taps into his own boundless passion
for rock and roll and acting and uses that

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:29.000
to guide his performance as the kung-fu-loving
Po.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.879
Much of the look of Kung Fu Panda 3 is based
on a trip the filmmakers took to China.

00:00:33.879 --> 00:00:37.989
For example, a lot of the architecture in
the Panda Village references what they saw

00:00:37.989 --> 00:00:42.560
during their visit to Mount Qing Cheng, an
important spiritual centre as well as a natural

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.000
home for pandas.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.809
The filmmakers also spent a couple of days
observing pandas at the Chengdu Panda Base,

00:00:49.809 --> 00:00:53.420
a research and breeding centre for giant pandas
in China.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:58.350
Director Jennifer Yuh Nelson even got to hold
a very sleepy baby panda on her lap!

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:02.880
The movie's creative crew then drew on their
observations of how pandas move and roll to

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.860
bring their own animated pandas to life.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:10.909
To get the right look for the movie's scroll
which tells the backstory of Oogway and Kai,

00:01:10.909 --> 00:01:14.910
the filmmakers took cues from a real-life
scroll called "Along the River During the

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:19.659
Qingming Festival", which is such an important
and famous work of art that it's said to be

00:01:19.659 --> 00:01:22.220
China's Mona Lisa.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:26.700
DreamWorks and their Shanghai-based sister
company Oriental DreamWorks worked together

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.900
to create two versions of Kung Fu Panda 3
with the same story and characters but with

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:36.290
the characters animated so that they speak
in sync with either English or Mandarin.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:41.479
More than 200 people worked on both versions
of the film at Oriental DreamWorks, and helped

00:01:41.479 --> 00:01:46.290
add to the movie's cultural authenticity,
for example, by advising on new female panda

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:53.360
Mei Mei's costume, and suggesting the inclusion
of traditional Chinese food in certain scenes.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.869
Jackie Chan, who voices Monkey in the English-language
Kung Fu Panda movies, also provides the voice

00:01:57.869 --> 00:02:03.060
of Po's biological father, Li, in the Mandarin
version of Kung Fu Panda 3.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.020
Speaking of Jackie Chan, because voice actors
for animated movies typically record their

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:11.800
parts separately, Kung Fu Panda star Jack
Black still hasn't met Jackie Chan despite

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:15.750
this being their third Kung Fu Panda movie
together!

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:19.930
Jack Black actually did some kung fu training
himself for the Kung Fu Panda movies, and

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:24.560
when he's recording his lines for Po's action
scenes, he likes to do a little kung fu in

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.750
the sound booth to make it sound more realistic.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:32.890
Before the Kung Fu Panda animators made their
first movie together, not only did they binge

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:37.690
on a ton of kung fu movies, but they also
took a kung fu class with top wushu coach

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:42.300
and stunt co-ordinator Eric Chen in order
to give them an insight into how the untrained

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:46.540
Po would feel facing someone accomplished
in martial arts.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:51.870
Animator and story artist Rodolphe Guenoden,
who's worked on all three Kung Fu Panda movies,

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:56.330
happens to have over 20 years' experience
with martial arts, so he's supervised the

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:58.400
kung fu choreography on all the films.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.890
He also continued the animation crew's martial
arts training with his own kung fu classes,

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:08.020
which helped the animators break down how
each character would move, for example how

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:11.480
they should position their feet or their hips.

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:16.450
To create the sound of Po flying and jumping,
the movie's sound editors lined up a load

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:22.090
of microphones along one of 20th Century Fox's
soundstages and then released a taut bungee

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:28.060
cord, which gave them "an incredible five-second-long
sound with an elastic and spongy quality."

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:34.850
When it came to the Spirit Realm, the movie's
sound editors used a variety of Chinese musical

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:39.670
instruments to generate the range of sounds
they wanted for Po's chi or energy force and

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:41.540
the villainous Kai's chi.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:46.560
But because many of the Spirit Realm scenes
are about inner stillness, they actually reduced

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.110
the number of sounds they used and concentrated
on just those sounds that they needed to show

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:54.130
the characters' emotional state.

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:58.510
To produce the sound of pandas bouncing and
rolling along, the sound editors made their

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:03.420
own musical instrument called a "gut-bucket",
which is essentially a large tin wash basin

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:08.560
that they turned upside down and cut a hole
in and then attached a stick and thread.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.830
For the movie's fight scenes, the sound editors
took the characteristic "whoosh" sounds you

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:19.350
hear in classic kung fu movies and expanded
on them, creating more than a thousand "whooshes"

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:22.120
for Kung Fu Panda 3.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:27.220
The original idea for the movie's jade warriors
came from Pan's Labyrinth and Hellboy director

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:31.510
Guillermo del Toro who is executive producer
for Kung Fu Panda 3.

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:35.970
And the design of the Master Porcupine jade
zombie goes all the way back to the first

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:41.790
Kung Fu Panda movie when the franchise's character
designer Nico Marlet did a sketch of a porcupine

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:46.670
doctor doing acupuncture using his own quills
as needles!

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:51.780
Angelina Jolie is back as the voice of Tigress,
and this time she's joined by four of her

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:57.280
children, Pax, Zahara, Shiloh, and Knox, who
cameo as characters including the panda triplets.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:01.880
While Knox's co-star siblings have already
appeared alongside their parents in other

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:06.050
movies, Kung Fu Panda 3 is actually Knox's
first big-screen performance.

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:11.930
But the Jolie-Pitt kids weren't the only ones
getting in on the Kung Fu Panda 3 act.

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:16.090
Jack Black's son Sammy provided the voice
of one of the movie's bunnies.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:21.720
And Dustin Hoffman's grandson Gus was super-excited
to voice one of the panda villager kids when

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.810
he visited his granddad during one of his
recording sessions for the film.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:31.880
Kate Hudson had such fun playing the super-confident,
ribbon-dancing panda Mei Mei that she broke

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:36.450
down laughing at the end of a couple of takes
that actually made it into the final movie.

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:41.040
To cover the sound of her laughter, the filmmakers
had to layer the end of her lines with the

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.050
cheering voices of the crowd!

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:47.780
In fact, Kate Hudson felt a special connection
to the movie because her own family had a

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:52.420
feng shui artist who spent a lot of time at
their home and also they often did tai chi

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:54.030
exercises as a family.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:59.890
Oh, and speaking of Mei Mei, she was originally
going to be played by Pitch Perfect star Rebel

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:04.340
Wilson, but Wilson had to drop out of the
movie when the production schedule was extended.

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:08.970
Hannibal star Mads Mikkelsen was also initially
set to play the movie's villain but bowed

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:14.420
out, after which the Oscar-winning star of
Whiplash JK Simmons joined the cast to play

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:16.640
the villainous Kai.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:21.020
Like many of the Kung Fu Panda team, editor
Clare Knight has worked on all three movies

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:22.380
in the franchise.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:24.930
But for Clare, it's been a real family affair.

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:29.460
While making the first movie, she met and
married her husband, actor Wayne Knight, who

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:31.020
voiced a role in the film.

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:35.560
Knight is probably best known as Newman in
the TV comedy Seinfeld and as Dennis Nedry

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:37.180
in Jurassic Park.

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:42.180
While making Kung Fu Panda 2, Clare gave birth
to her son, Liam, who provides the voice of

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:44.100
Baby Po in the movie.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:48.930
And Clare's husband and son are both back
voicing Big Fun, Hom-Lee, and Lei Lei for

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:50.620
Kung Fu Panda 3.

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:55.650
Remember the first Kung Fu Panda movie, which
opened with Po imagining himself as a hero

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:59.090
so deadly that his enemies would go blind
from pure awesomeness?

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:06.470
Well, there's a cool call-back to that opening
sequence in Kung Fu Panda 3's climactic confrontation.

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:11.130
While making the movie, Alessandro Carloni,
who co-directed Kung Fu Panda 3 with Jennifer

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:14.490
Yuh Nelson, decided to take a leaf of out
of Po's book.

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:18.400
So, when the movie's animators showed him
something he really liked, instead of just

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:23.080
okaying it and moving on, he jumped around
enthusiastically, embracing the kind of passion

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:24.770
Po shows in the films.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:29.389
And one of director Alessandro Carloni's favourite
scenes in the movie takes place between Po

00:07:29.389 --> 00:07:31.750
and his father Li in the Hall of Heroes.

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:36.620
Li tries on a suit of armour and playfully
headbutts Po in the side, which reminded Carloni

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:38.980
of a moment he shared with his own father.

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:42.740
Carloni is really into motorbikes and one
day he and his dad put on motorbike helmets

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:45.730
and his dad started playing around bumping
into him.

